--- !u!114 &1582174451
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1582174449}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'An electric switch is a device used to control the flow of electric current
    in an electrical circuit. It works by either completing or breaking the conducting
    path.


    When the switch is ON, its contacts are connected and the circuit
    becomes closed. This provides a continuous path through which electric current
    can flow. For example, in a simple circuit containing a battery, switch, wires,
    and bulb, closing the switch allows current to reach the bulb and the bulb lights
    up.


    When the switch is OFF, its contacts are separated and the circuit
    becomes open. The conducting path is broken, so current cannot flow through the
    complete circuit and the bulb turns off.


    A switch does not produce electrical
    energy. Instead, it controls whether the electrical circuit is complete or incomplete.
    Common examples include wall switches used for lights, push-button switches,
    toggle switches, and switches used in electrical appliances.


    In a 3D learning
    model, the operation of a switch can be clearly demonstrated by showing the actual
    movement of its contacts. When the student turns the switch ON, the contacts
    join, current-flow animation starts, and the bulb illuminates. When the student
    turns it OFF, the contacts separate, current-flow animation stops, and the bulb
    goes dark.


    This simple ON/OFF operation helps students understand an important
    principle of electricity: a complete conducting path is required for current
    to flow in a basic circuit.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_fontSharedMaterials: []
  m_fontMaterial: {fileID: 0}
